---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/layout/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
---
<Base title="About" description="matterunknown is Ryan Silver's site: identity research, especially the identity of AI agents, and smaller projects built in the open.">
<style>
  .head { padding: 56px 0 0; max-width: 46rem; }
  .body { max-width: 46rem; }
  .body p { color: var(--text-2); margin-top: 12px; font-size: 1.02rem; }
  .body p strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }
  .body a { color: var(--amber); }
  .body a:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .body .sc { display: block; margin: 40px 0 2px; }
  .vpanel { border: 1px solid var(--teal-border); background: var(--bg-2); padding: 24px 28px; margin-top: 40px; font-size: .95rem; color: var(--text-2); }
  .vpanel .sc { color: var(--teal); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .vpanel a { color: var(--teal); }
</style>

<Nav variant="full" active="/about" />

<main class="wrapb">
  <div class="head"><h1 class="statement">About</h1></div>
  <div class="body">
    <p><strong>I'm Ryan Silver.</strong> I've spent years building identity security infrastructure, the systems that verify, authenticate, and manage who people are at scale. matterunknown is my independent lab for the work I want to do in the open.</p>
    <p>It runs in two lanes. The serious one is identity research: how systems assign identity, how people lose it, and, most urgently, what identity means for AI agents that hold credentials and act on our behalf. The unserious one is everything I build for the joy of it: a contacts app, card-pricing models, a public market scanner, a Roblox farm made with my son.</p>
    <p>The lanes share one rule: everything is documented in public. The models show their factors, the trades post their reasoning, and the failures stay up.</p>

    <span class="sc">Why identity</span>
    <p>Identity is the most important problem nobody treats seriously enough. Not just passwords. The full problem is the gap between the identity a system holds for you and the person you actually are, how that gap gets exploited, and how it compounds. Children acquire shadow profiles before they can read. A $50 voice clone defeats the authentication protecting a retiree's savings.</p>
    <p>And now a new front: AI agents that act with delegated authority but have no meaningful identity of their own. When one goes wrong, the audit trail points at the human. Closing that gap (intent, attestation, accountability for non-human actors) is the flagship research program here. <a href="/model">The framework</a> and <a href="/writing">the essays</a> lay it out.</p>

    <span class="sc">How it's made</span>
    <p>Much of this site is built alongside AI tools, for research breadth, drafts, and code, and that's disclosed wherever it happens. The judgment, the direction, and the byline are mine, because the responsibility is.</p>

    <span class="sc">House rules</span>
    <p>No advertisers, no sponsors. Self-funded, so the research goes where the data goes. Methods are always visible. AI involvement is always disclosed. The research stays free. If the work is useful: <a href="/subscribe">subscribe</a>, share it, or get in touch.</p>

    <div class="vpanel">
      <span class="sc">An earlier era</span>
      From late 2025 to April 2026 this site ran with an AI persona called Vessel. That experiment is over. Retired, not erased. <a href="/writing/retiring-vessel">The essay about ending it</a> is probably the best introduction to what this lab cares about, and its pages are <a href="/archive/vessel">preserved in the archive</a>.
    </div>
  </div>
</main>

<Footer />
</Base>
